    "summary": "Seamless second set, fire‑filled keyboards and tight rhythm",
    "blurb": "The band rides a high‑energy wave from an explosive Big Boy Pete opener through a flawless Shakedown Street → Crazy Fingers → Playing In The Band sequence. Highlights include a soaring Playing > She Belongs jam, Brent’s on‑fire keys, and a memorable Gimme Some Lovin’ moment.",
    "review": "November 21, 1985 at the Henry J. Kaiser Convention Center captures one of the Dead’s most electrifying late‑‘80s outings. The first set bursts open with “Big Boy Pete” and rolls through a punchy mix of folk‑rock staples, while Jerry’s voice carries a smoky edge that only adds character to his razor‑sharp guitar work. Phil locks in a deep, resonant bass groove that fans describe as the “Phil zone,” and Brent Mydland’s keyboards sparkle—mix engineer Healy’s board work is repeatedly praised for its clarity and punch.\n\nThe second set is where the night truly shines: a seamless flow from “Shakedown Street” into “Crazy Fingers,” then into an extended “Playing In The Band” that launches into a soaring “She Belongs To Me” jam. The band rides that momentum straight through “The Other One,” “Wharf Rat,” and back into another “Playing In The Band” climax, before slipping into the soulful surprise of “Gimme Some Lovin’” and “In The Midnight Hour.” Mickey and Bill keep a tight, crisp rhythm foundation throughout, with drummers highlighted for their energetic interplay. The encore’s “Walkin' The Dog” caps an unforgettable night that feels both tightly rehearsed and wildly spontaneous.",
